--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OCR-stage structure (ocr_only_5090.py, STAGE4):
  Three engines run PER PAGE, SERIALLY, in this order (lines 466-486):
    1. Tesseract   (run_tesseract)  -- CPU engine (pytesseract, --oem 1 --psm 6)
    2. docTR       (run_doctr)      -- GPU engine (model.cuda(), torch.inference_mode)
    3. Surya       (run_surya)      -- GPU engine (Detection+Recognition predictors)
  Then a token-set 2-of-3 consensus (CPU, cheap).
  Timing: only the COMBINED per-page time is logged (ocr_timings, "seconds" per page).
  There is NO per-engine timing in the log -- per-engine split is CODE-STRUCTURE estimate only.

  Tesseract CPU-share estimate (from structure, not measured):
    Tesseract on a ~300-DPI full page (--psm 6) typically runs ~0.8-1.5 s/page CPU.
    docTR + Surya (two deep models, single-image/un-batched, GPU) dominate the ~3.0 s/page.
    Rough split: Tesseract ~0.8-1.2s (CPU), docTR+Surya ~1.8-2.2s (GPU). => Tesseract is
    on the order of ~30-40% of OCR-stage WALL time but it is CPU-side, so during OCR the
    5090 CPU is NOT idle -- it carries Tesseract serially interleaved with the GPU engines.

  LIVE CPU/GPU-during-OCR sampling: SKIPPED. At measurement time the 5090 was BETWEEN
  volumes (nvidia-smi: util 0%, mem 6633 MiB) -- no worker in OCR phase (>8GB / >40%)
  within the window, so per the brief this sub-step was skipped rather than waited on.

================================================================================
[2026-06-02 ~19:12 PT] MEASUREMENT 2 -- handoff cost (one real transfer test)
================================================================================
Representative completed volume: production-1907-09 (OCR DONE 17:56, not in active queue).
  pages_prep_gray:   1403 files, 1,351,921,631 bytes = 1289.3 MB
  page_classification.json: 14,479 bytes (negligible)

ONE real test (tar+gzip on 5090 -> scp -O over Tailscale -> 3060 D:\PatoLex-archive):
  COMPRESS (tar -czf): 37.1 s  -> 1,015,793,202 bytes = 968.7 MB  (only ~25% shrink;
                       inputs are already-binarized grayscale PNGs, near-incompressible)
  TRANSFER (scp -O, id_5090_to_3060 key, untrottled Tailscale): 16.7 s
  EFFECTIVE THROUGHPUT: 968.7 MB / 16.7 s = ~58 MB/s  (~464 Mbit/s over Tailscale)
  Integrity: byte-exact on 3060 (1,015,793,202 == source). SCP_EXIT=0.
  CLEANUP: tarball deleted on BOTH 3060 and 5090; Test-Path=False both ends. No stray files.

  Handoff total (compress 37.1s + transfer 16.7s) = ~53.8 s per ~1150-1400-page volume.
  (If transfer of raw uncompressed dir at 58 MB/s instead: 1289 MB/58 = ~22s, NO 37s compress
   -- i.e. skipping compression is FASTER end-to-end given near-incompressible PNGs: ~22s vs ~54s.)

HANDOFF vs PREP-SAVED verdict:
  Prep-stage time that offloading would REMOVE from the 5-series box, per volume (median):
    render ~76s + preprocess ~1080s + classify ~0 = ~1156 s (~19 min) of 5090 CPU work.
  Handoff cost to ship the prepped pages back: ~54s compressed (or ~22s raw).
  => Handoff is CHEAP: ~5% of the prep time it offloads (or ~2% uncompressed).
     The make-or-break number passes comfortably -- moving prep to the 3060 buys back
     ~19 min/volume of 5-series time for ~1min of transfer.
     CAVEAT: this is the cost to move PREPPED pages 3060->5090. A full role-split also
     needs the SOURCE PDF on the 3060 (one-time, PDFs are tens of MB) -- also cheap.

================================================================================
DESIGN READ (which the numbers favor)
================================================================================
The pipeline is ~75% OCR (GPU-bound, ~3.0 s/pg) and ~25% prep (CPU-bound, ~1.0 s/pg,
of which preprocessing is 94%). Handoff is ~1 min/volume vs ~19 min prep saved -- cheap.
BUT prep currently runs INTERLEAVED with OCR across 3-4 concurrent workers, so the prep
CPU cost is already partially hidden behind GPU OCR; the headline 25% is wall-time per
volume in isolation, not necessarily idle GPU. The real contention is that Tesseract
(a CPU engine, ~30-40% of OCR-stage wall, serial per page) competes with preprocess for
the SAME CPU. That means **prep-offload alone does NOT free the 5-series CPU** -- Tesseract
keeps the CPU busy throughout OCR.

Favored design: a STAGED role-split, not a single lever.
  1. Move render+preprocess+classify to the 3060 (CPU-cheap on GPU box, handoff trivial).
     The 3060 preps ahead; 5090/5080 pull prepped pages and run GPU OCR. Frees ~19min/vol
     of 5-series CPU AND removes preprocess<->Tesseract CPU contention during OCR.
  2. Strongly consider ALSO moving Tesseract to the 3060 (or dropping it to a 2-of-2
     docTR+Surya consensus on the GPU box) -- because Tesseract is the CPU load that
     survives prep-offload. Tesseract on the 3060 can run while the 5090 does docTR+Surya,
     then its text ships back with the prepped pages (text is tiny). This makes the
     5-series boxes GPU-ONLY -- the cleanest win.
  Intra-worker pipelining (overlap prep of vol N+1 with OCR of vol N on the same box)
  is the weakest option: it leaves both prep AND Tesseract on the 5-series CPU and only
  hides latency, not load.
  RECOMMENDATION: full role-split (3060 = render+preprocess+classify+Tesseract; 5-series
  = docTR+Surya GPU consensus only). Numbers support it; handoff cost is noise.
  NOTE: the 3060's own throughput at ~1.0 s/pg prep + ~1.0 s/pg Tesseract = ~2 s/pg CPU
  must keep up with two GPU boxes consuming ~3.0 s/pg EACH across 3-4 workers -- the 3060
  may become the new bottleneck; size that before committing (out of scope for this run).
